    "['ptp delay-mechanism [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp interface <interface-name> delay-mechanism pdelay', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ptp interface <interface-name> delay-mechanism pdelay\\n##\\n\\nNote: In the Juniper command, you would replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., `ge-1/0/0` or `ge-2/0/0`) where you want to apply the PTP delay mechanism. The Cisco command is interface-specific, so the Juniper equivalent would also need to be applied to specific interfaces.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp interface <interface-name> delay-mechanism pdelay'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\nset interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, \"disable\" is the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"shutdown\" command. Therefore, to achieve the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" (which enables the interface), we would actually need to *remove* the \"disable\" statement in Juniper. However, since you asked for a direct translation of the command, I\\'ve provided the inverse form. \\n\\nThe more accurate functional equivalent in Juniper would be to simply not have the \"disable\" statement (as interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper), but this is how the direct command translation would look.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\nset interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, \"disable\" is the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"shutdown\" command. Therefore, to achieve the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" (which enables the interface), we would actually need to *remove* the \"disable\" statement in Juniper. However, since you asked for a direct translation of the command, I\\'ve provided the inverse form. \\n\\nThe more accurate functional equivalent in Juniper would be to simply not have the \"disable\" statement (as interfaces are enabled by default in Juniper), but this is how the direct command translation would look.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['ptp delay-mechanism [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp delay-mechanism pdelay', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent command for `ptp delay-mechanism pdelay`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ptp delay-mechanism pdelay\\n##\\n\\nThis command configures the PTP (Precision Time Protocol) to use peer delay measurement mechanism on a Juniper router, which is equivalent to the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp delay-mechanism pdelay'}]]": 1,
